This is a robust multi-modal automation task blending text extraction, multi-image analysis, and structured cross-referencing with categorical validation schemas.Here is a recommended architectural blueprint and step-by-step implementation strategy to build this system cleanly and fulfill all evaluation and operational requirements.🛠️ System Architecture StrategyTo handle the rate limits, token costs, and multi-modal constraints, a multi-stage pipeline is highly recommended over a single massive prompt.[claims.csv + user_history.csv] ──> Step 1: Text Parse & Rule Match ──> Step 2: Multi-Modal Vision Analysis ──> Step 3: Structured Validation ──> [output.csv]
Step 1: Claim Extraction & Rule Matching (Text Only)Goal: Parse user_claim to identify the reported issue family and lookup the minimum evidence needed from evidence_requirements.csv. Look up the user's risk flags from user_history.csv.Optimization: Doing this via text-only saves vision token costs and helps form a highly directed prompt for the vision model.Step 2: Multi-Modal Vision Analysis (Vision + Text)Goal: Pass the extracted target claim, object type, evidence rules, and the actual images (image_paths) to a Vision LLM (e.g., GPT-4o or Claude 3.5 Sonnet).Prompt Strategy: Instruct the model to evaluate the image strictly as the primary source of truth. Provide it with the list of Allowed Values for issue_type, object_part, and risk_flags so it does not hallucinate custom categories.Step 3: Enforcer & Schema Serialization (Code Only)Goal: Take the output from the vision model, strictly map it to the requested CSV schema, ensure invalid values default cleanly (e.g., to unknown or none), extract image IDs (stripping extensions), and format semicolons.💻 Recommended Project StructureYour code.zip should be organized cleanly to separate data, logic, and your required evaluation components:Plaintext├── dataset/
│   ├── claims.csv
│   ├── sample_claims.csv
│   ├── user_history.csv
│   ├── evidence_requirements.csv
│   └── images/
├── evaluation/
│   ├── evaluation_report.md     <-- Required Operational Analysis
│   └── sample_evaluation_results.csv
├── src/
│   ├── __init__.py
│   ├── pipeline.py              <-- Orchestrates the processing loop
│   ├── text_processor.py        <-- Merges history and requirements
│   └── vision_analyst.py        <-- Handles Multi-modal API calls
├── README.md
└── main.py                      <-- Script to execute processing
📊 Operational Analysis & Cost Estimator BlueprintFor your mandatory evaluation/evaluation_report.md, you can structure your analysis using this breakdown template:1. Cost & Token AssumptionsVision Cost: Standard Vision APIs typically charge per $512 \times 512$ tile. Assume an average of 800–1100 tokens per image for high-res detail parsing.Text Cost: Input context (prompt, history, conversation) $\approx 600$ tokens. Structured output $\approx 250$ tokens.Pricing Formula:$$\text{Cost} = (\text{Total Input Tokens} \times \text{Input Price}) + (\text{Total Output Tokens} \times \text{Output Price})$$2. Efficiency & Reliability StrategiesImage Batching/Parallelization: Use an asynchronous worker pool (asyncio or ThreadPoolExecutor) to run parallel image checks to respect latency limits without hitting rate limits (RPM).Caching: Cache images that appear in multiple claims or pre-hash them to avoid redundant processing if identical files are uploaded.Exponential Backoff: Wrap the model pipeline in a retry mechanism (like the tenacity library) to catch 429 Rate Limit Exceeded errors gracefully.🛡️ Edge Cases to Handle in Code⚠️ Strict Constraint Reminders:Image ID Extraction: Ensure your code converts images/test/case_001/img_1.jpg down to just img_1 for the supporting_image_ids column.Fallback Handling: If an issue type or object part is ambiguous, strictly fall back to unknown as specified by the allowed values list. Do not let the model generate words like "car_door" instead of door.